//
// A CAPTURING CLOSURE IS NOT AN ELEMENT OF A COLLECTION (`@FR-L-Escape`).  L-Escape names where
// a closure is a value: a variable, a struct field, an argument, a return.  A collection is not
// on the list, by decision (loft: DESIGN_DECISIONS @C116): a collection has one element layout
// and each capture set is its own record shape.  The refusal names the two cures — a struct
// field, or a non-capturing fn reading its state from a field (the dispatch table
// `L-FnRef/a-keyed-collection-of-fn-refs-dispatches-by-name` builds).  The OCaml bar's B1.
//
// Each capturing element is refused on its own, so the two below answer two expectations.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: a capturing closure cannot be stored in a collection
// @EXPECT_ERROR: a capturing closure cannot be stored in a collection
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; the refusal fires on 9121f1b50, interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile diagnostic.
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names the cure.
// HOLDS: `1874-a-fn-field-holds-any-closure` beside it, the admitted home.

fn main() {
  base = 10;
  scale = 3;
  steps: vector<fn(integer) -> integer> = [
    fn(x: integer) -> integer { x + base },
    fn(x: integer) -> integer { x * scale },
  ];
  v = 1;
  for f in steps { v = f(v); }
  assert(v == 33, "unreachable: {v}");
}
